                                <subviews>
                                    <view contentMode="scaleToFill" translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints="NO" id="6CH-nC-UhR">
                                        <rect key="frame" x="0.0" y="0.0" width="378" height="1080"/>
                                        <subviews>
                                            <textView clipsSubviews="YES" multipleTouchEnabled="YES" contentMode="scaleToFill" editable="NO" translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints="NO" id="Lnm-0x-Lga">
                                                <rect key="frame" x="10" y="10" width="358" height="1070"/>
                                                <color key="backgroundColor" white="0.0" alpha="0.0" colorSpace="custom" customColorSpace="genericGamma22GrayColorSpace"/>
                                                <string key="text">20 years ago my first daughter was 2 years old. I was new to East Vancouver and was stunned and touched by the abundance of new mom and baby programs available during a time of such personal and emotional flux. I made it my mission to get the word out through a print map, window decals for family-friendly businesses and a website. Mommy Map was born, and even thrived. (Read about it here in the 2006 Globe and Mail article)
 Here we are two decades later. Paper maps are mostly the things of fairytales, parenting has gotten way hipper, and conversations have gotten way more honest and nuanced about times of flux and family roles and dynamics. The word “Mommy”, in fact, is even somewhat outmoded. Yet here I tread, embarking on Mommy Map’s reboot as an app.
 Even as so much of life has shifted online (especially after CoVid), it seems deeply important, if not more so, to highlight real world spaces that receive us (as any type of human with baby in tow) with the four key amenities of family-friendliness: diaper changing areas, breast feeding cozy areas, play areas and kids menus. As far as I can foretell, the very real world dilemma of a full diaper will not ever be solved by AI, and nor will a baby’s preference for quiet whilst feeding.
 I now split my life between Vancouver and Santa Fe, NM, and for this reason you will see these two places as my starting locales. I look forward to expanding to include more cities soon, especially the ones that seem the least family-friendly because these, of course, can feel the most daunting and isolating for the parents of new babies.
 Who Made This? Mommy Map is run by Dalit Holzman and her daughter Starla Smeeth. (App Developed by Getyoteam Solution llp)
 Feedback Let us know if you have a place to suggest, or run into incorrect info or any bugs in the app, or if you just want to connect.</string>
                                                <color key="textColor" systemColor="labelColor"/>
                                                <fontDescription key="fontDescription" name="Comfortaa-SemiBold" family="Comfortaa" pointSize="16"/>
                                                <textInputTraits key="textInputTraits" autocapitalizationType="sentences"/>
                                                <dataDetectorType key="dataDetectorTypes" link="YES"/>
                                            </textView>
                                        </subviews>
